1
00:00:00,325 --> 00:00:02,875
You tap a box that says
you're not a robot.

2
00:00:03,091 --> 00:00:06,716
Researchers say the test started
before your finger got there.

3
00:00:07,292 --> 00:00:10,442
For years, the test was
reading wobbly letters.

4
00:00:10,685 --> 00:00:15,025
In twenty thirteen, Google said its
risk analysis covers everything you do

5
00:00:15,035 --> 00:00:18,510
around a challenge, not
only the answer you type.

6
00:00:18,916 --> 00:00:22,732
In twenty fourteen, the checkbox
arrived, and many people passed

7
00:00:22,742 --> 00:00:24,066
with one click.

8
00:00:24,623 --> 00:00:26,488
The click feels like the test.

9
00:00:26,498 --> 00:00:28,748
It's really a request for a verdict.

10
00:00:29,046 --> 00:00:31,086
The evidence was gathered around it.

11
00:00:31,096 --> 00:00:33,061
If it looks human, you pass.

12
00:00:33,071 --> 00:00:35,296
If it can't tell, you get a puzzle.

13
00:00:35,918 --> 00:00:37,233
So what does it watch?

14
00:00:37,243 --> 00:00:39,468
Google doesn't publish the full list.

15
00:00:39,744 --> 00:00:43,034
A twenty twenty-one survey
describes the checkbox recording

16
00:00:43,044 --> 00:00:47,369
cursor movement, where you click,
and how long the pointer hovers.

17
00:00:47,609 --> 00:00:50,874
It also records browser
details and cookies.

18
00:00:50,884 --> 00:00:55,059
Google's own FAQ calls a cookie
essential to that analysis.

19
00:00:55,630 --> 00:00:58,780
A real hand never moves
at one steady speed.

20
00:00:58,930 --> 00:01:02,080
It speeds up, peaks,
and eases into the target.

21
00:01:02,387 --> 00:01:05,652
In nineteen eighty-five, Flash
and Hogan modeled arm movement

22
00:01:05,662 --> 00:01:09,287
this way: a smooth,
bell-shaped curve of speed.

23
00:01:09,683 --> 00:01:14,183
One published detector checks cursor
paths against a model like that.

24
00:01:14,579 --> 00:01:18,019
In a twenty twenty-four study
of reCAPTCHA version two,

25
00:01:18,029 --> 00:01:21,754
any cursor movement cut the number
of puzzles needed to pass.

26
00:01:22,065 --> 00:01:25,965
Straight paths and curved ones
were not significantly different.

27
00:01:26,288 --> 00:01:27,503
So is it the wobble?

28
00:01:27,513 --> 00:01:29,013
Google doesn't say.

29
00:01:29,601 --> 00:01:31,276
Then there's the browser.

30
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:36,066
A twenty twenty-four study reports
that cookies and browsing history carry

31
00:01:36,076 --> 00:01:39,600
substantial weight in whether
a visitor seems human.

32
00:01:39,943 --> 00:01:42,783
Browsing history is
what researchers report.

33
00:01:42,793 --> 00:01:44,693
Google hasn't confirmed it.

34
00:01:45,294 --> 00:01:47,944
Version three has no checkbox at all.

35
00:01:48,142 --> 00:01:50,606
It returns a score from zero to one.

36
00:01:50,616 --> 00:01:53,206
One means very likely a good interaction.

37
00:01:53,216 --> 00:01:55,542
Zero means very likely a bot.

38
00:01:55,908 --> 00:02:00,583
Each site sets its own cutoff, and
Google suggests starting at point five.

39
00:02:00,921 --> 00:02:02,661
But a score can be pushed.

40
00:02:02,671 --> 00:02:06,936
In twenty nineteen, researchers trained
an AI agent to move and click in a

41
00:02:06,946 --> 00:02:08,496
simulator they built.

42
00:02:08,746 --> 00:02:12,861
It reached a score of point nine about
ninety-seven percent of the time.

43
00:02:12,871 --> 00:02:15,246
The simulator isn't the live site.

44
00:02:15,823 --> 00:02:19,688
A twenty twenty-three study ran
for thirteen months with more than

45
00:02:19,698 --> 00:02:21,573
thirty-six hundred people.

46
00:02:21,820 --> 00:02:26,510
Its authors judged the checkbox
costly, with close to no security gain,

47
00:02:26,520 --> 00:02:28,495
and say it should be retired.

48
00:02:29,046 --> 00:02:32,362
Nobody outside Google knows
how the signals are weighted.

49
00:02:32,372 --> 00:02:34,946
So every study tests from the outside.

50
00:02:35,550 --> 00:02:38,250
So the tap is the last step, not the test.

51
00:02:38,756 --> 00:02:40,531
It cares how you got there.
